# H1 before, unpatched pin. Comparable row for a later after run.
# Not the same row as harness/logs/before-partial.txt (commit d0fcfc6a): that file is a
# 1280x360 two-colour fixture, and part of it ran while the edge failed to bind.
# Host: Framework5070, Arch Linux 7.2.14-arch1-1 x86_64. Not Ubuntu.
# rustc 1.95.0 (59807616e 2026-04-14). hostname is not installed; address recorded from `ip`.
# LAN 192.168.0.108/24 on wlp191s0. Tailscale on the same box: 100.121.90.25.
# Pin moq 4a6f522483168ace392718d68b58a402533c897d (short 4a6f52248). moq-webrtc from git, not vendored. Policy off.
# Binaries: /tmp/kf-h1/debug/{edge,harness}, built earlier with CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0 and CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0.
# Browser: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, /usr/bin/chromium, Playwright 1.65.1 at /tmp/kf-pw, headless.
# Fixture: ffmpeg testsrc2 1280x720 rate 30, drawtext frame number (NotoSans-Regular), sine 440 Hz.
# Route: in-process relay on the Framework, edge listen 127.0.0.1:8088, ICE bound 192.168.0.108:40000,
# advertised 192.168.0.108:40001 when the loss proxy was in front. edge_up=yes on every section below.
# Boundary: browser firstPresentedMs is WHEP POST (window.lastPostAt) to requestVideoFrameCallback.
# MoQ rows are harness raw-frame payload bytes, plus ffmpeg decode of an ivf written only when raw runs to its deadline.
# UDP buffer warning (wanted 8388608, granted 4194304) was logged and left alone. No firewall or sysctl change.
# Scratch /tmp/kf-h1 and /tmp/kf-before720 kept. /tmp/kf-before (the 360p partial) was not used.

## A — meets the written target on this fixture
WHEP POST to first presented frame. 10 joins each. Picture on every join: videoWidth 1280, videoHeight 720, audioPeakHz 439 except two VP8 samples at 434.
H.264 answer codecs were H264 x6 plus opus. firstPresentedMs: 400 345 406 384 376 343 413 413 377 359.
Sorted 343 345 359 376 377 384 400 406 413 413. Median 380.5 ms. Max 413 ms.
VP8 answer codecs VP8 plus opus. firstPresentedMs: 564 367 467 428 515 511 448 494 424 164.
Sorted 164 367 424 428 448 467 494 511 515 564. Median 457.5 ms. Max 564 ms.
Join 10 connectMs was 4 and firstPresentedMs 164; the picture was still 1280x720 at 439 Hz.
Both medians are under 2.0 s and both maxima under 2.5 s. The after run still has to show the cause, not treat this as adoption.

## B — first payload is fast; the 10-join lag does not meet 2.2 s
Publisher is the same 1280x720 VP8 ffmpeg pipe. Ten late subscribes, each killed at the first raw-frame, so those runs wrote no ivf.
first_raw_s: 0.105 0.120 0.066 0.062 0.101 0.035 0.037 0.077 0.068 0.081. All under 1.0 s.
The first four were the same payload (group_start=0, 14181 bytes, media=1s). The next six were the same payload (18072 bytes, media=2s).
The publisher had already been running for about 4 s, so those groups are about 2–4 s behind the live edge. That misses lag ≤ 2.2 s.
A later hold on a fresh publisher, same binary and fixture, let raw run 3 s so the ivf was written:
ffprobe b-decode.ivf: vp8 1280x720, 102 frames, duration 3.40 s, about 1549 kb/s.
First raw-frame of that hold: media=4s wall_ms=2, which matches the 4 s preroll (near live, not the cached 1 s group).
Decoded frame /tmp/kf-before720/frames/b0.png is 1280x720. The frame-counter box has 618 bright pixels (sample 201,34,54), so the counter is present.
B's subscribe time meets 1.0 s. B's 10-join lag does not meet 2.2 s. Do not call B a pass.

